#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""health: the machine's health as a harmonograph, live in the terminal.

One Python file, standard library only. Every frame draws the trace of a damped
two-pendulum harmonograph whose frequencies, phases, damping and colours are set by the
machine's readings at that moment, as large as the window, redrawn about ten times a
second until q.

How the readings set the curve (documented here, deliberately not on screen). The
picture is the one on an oscilloscope in X-Y mode: one pendulum drives X, the other Y.
Two signals locked at a simple ratio, at a fixed phase, with no loss, draw one closed
Lissajous curve that stands still and retraces itself. That is the idle machine. Every
reading is a departure from that picture, each in its own way, and every departure is
zero when the reading is at idle:

    hour of day       which locked ratio (2:3, 3:4, 3:5, 4:5, 5:6, 5:7): the family of
                      the figure; the clock rather than health
    uptime            the phase between X and Y: the figure turns slowly as the machine
                      runs; the clock rather than health
    cpu temperature   detuning: the ratio slips, the figure precesses and fills in
                      (35 C locked, 80 C a slip of 2.5 rad over the trace, squared so a
                      warm machine barely slips); and the hue, 35 C blue to 80 C red
    load per core     damping: each lap smaller, the figure spirals in (idle retraces
                      itself; at one load per core the last lap is 40 %)
    memory in use     the second pendulum: a wobble riding on the clean curve (none at
                      0 %, as large as the main term at 100 %)
    disk used         the colour spread along the trace: one hue when the disk is
                      empty, the whole wheel when it is full
    user@host         seed of the fine phases, stable per account
    second            phase of the wobble

So a quiet machine draws one closed figure in one cool colour, and a stressed machine a
drifting, shrinking, wobbling tangle across the whole wheel.

The readings reach the figure with inertia (temperature and memory over about three
seconds, load over two, the hour's ratio over about a minute), so the figure never
shivers with the sensor and glides from one family to the next.

On a terminal that draws pixels the frame is a PNG (rendered here, no library) sent over
the Kitty graphics protocol (Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm, also through tmux) or the iTerm2
inline-image protocol, or as sixels through `chafa` when it is installed (Windows
Terminal, foot, mlterm). Anywhere else, or with --braille, the trace is Braille dots.

Usage: health [FPS] [--braille] [--once] [--png PATH] [--demo DIR] ...; see --help.
"""
